Study help

Flashcard Template Maker (index.html)
-------------------------------------
A single file web app. Open index.html in any modern browser. It works offline,
with no build step, no backend and no external libraries.

1. Build prompt: enter a subject, level, card count, card types and optional
   exam or notes, then copy the research prompt.
2. Paste the prompt into any LLM (Claude, ChatGPT, Gemini). It returns JSON
   (schema version 1, included in the prompt).
3. Import: paste or upload the JSON. Code fences and stray text are removed,
   the deck is validated, and problems are listed in plain language.
4. Cards: preview, flip, filter, untick, edit inline and export JSON.
5. Study order: topics sorted by prerequisites, with loops flagged, plus a
   printable one page study order sheet.
6. Print: A4 with cut lines and crop marks, 2 x 4 or 3 x 3 per page.
   Double-sided mode mirrors the back pages for duplex printing (flip on
   long edge). Single-sided fold mode prints front and back side by side.
7. Play: test yourself on screen with the loaded deck. Choose a topic,
   difficulty and length, then pick a challenge:
   - Quiz: multiple choice, up to four options per card.
   - Beat the clock: multiple choice against a 60, 90 or 120 second timer.
     Each wrong answer costs 5 seconds.
   - Sudden death: get through every chosen card on 3 lives.
   - Match up: pair cards with their answers, 5 to a board, with a speed bonus.
   - Type it: type the missing words of cloze cards, the term for a
     definition, or a short answer. Small typos are forgiven, and an
     "I was right" button overrides a strict mark.
   Answers in a row build a streak multiplier (x2, then x3). Results list the
   cards you missed, with a button to practise just those. Best scores are
   kept per deck and challenge.

   Voice mode (tick it on the Play tab) reads each question and its options
   aloud and lets you answer by speaking: say the option number ("two",
   "option 3") or the answer itself. Say "repeat" to hear it again, "skip"
   to pass, "next" to move on, or "I was right" to overrule a Type it mark.
   It is hands-free: after the feedback is read out, the next card starts on
   its own. Beat the clock pauses its timer while a question is being read.
   Match up is read out but played by clicking. Reading aloud works in all
   modern browsers. Listening needs Chrome, Edge or Safari and an internet
   connection, because the browser sends the audio to its speech service.
   The browser asks for microphone access the first time.

Natural voices with VoiceStudio (optional)
------------------------------------------
For lifelike voices and private, more accurate listening, voice mode can use
VoiceStudio (https://github.com/debpalash/VoiceStudio), a free app that runs
speech models on your own computer.

1. Install VoiceStudio and open it. It serves a speech API at
   http://127.0.0.1:3900.
2. Start this app with the launcher: python3 serve.py
   It opens http://localhost:8765 and passes voice requests to VoiceStudio.
   Browsers do not let a page opened as a file talk to VoiceStudio, so use
   the launcher for this. It needs Python 3.7 or later and nothing else.
   Options: --port 9000, --voicestudio http://127.0.0.1:3900
3. In Play, tick Voice mode, set Voice engine to VoiceStudio and press
   Connect. Pick one of your cloned or designed voices and a speed.

With VoiceStudio:
- Questions are read in a natural voice. The next card is prepared while you
  answer, so there is little waiting.
- Your answer is recorded only while you speak, then transcribed by
  VoiceStudio on your computer. Nothing is sent to the internet. The deck's
  own words are passed as a hint, so subject terms are recognised well.
- "Answer while it reads" lets you cut a question short by speaking. Use
  headphones for this, so the microphone does not hear the speakers.
- If VoiceStudio stops responding, the game carries on with the browser's
  voice and recognition.
VoiceStudio is AGPL-3.0 licensed and its voice models have their own terms.
Only clone voices you have permission to use.

The last deck, settings and best scores are kept in the browser's local storage.
